    "openrouter_cost_usd": 0.007391874,
    "reply": "The available evidence spans both the molecular landscape of EGFR variant annotations and active clinical investigation in non-small cell lung cancer (NSCLC). ClinVar contains numerous EGFR missense variants deposited on transcript NM_005228.5 [6]-[30], while ClinicalTrials.gov lists multiple interventional studies addressing EGFR-driven NSCLC through tyrosine kinase inhibitors and emerging cellular therapies [1]-[5].\n\n## EGFR Variants in ClinVar\n\nThe ClinVar archive catalogs diverse EGFR substitutions across the receptor's functional domains. These include classical activating mutations such as p.Asn280Lys (ClinVar:1000076) [6] and p.Arg222Cys (ClinVar:1000203) [7], alongside variants of uncertain significance like p.Val1011Leu (ClinVar:1002578) [20] and p.Ser1070Asn (ClinVar:1002794) [21]. The collection encompasses alterations affecting kinase domain residues (p.Thr605Ala, ClinVar:1000851) [10], extracellular ligand-binding regions (p.Arg165Gln, ClinVar:1000496) [8], and C-terminal regulatory segments [28]-[30]. No clinical significance assertions accompany these records in the retrieved set.\n\n## Active Therapeutic Trials\n\nFive NSCLC trials explicitly target EGFR-mutant populations. First-generation EGFR-TKI strategies are represented by gefitinib and erlotinib retrospective analyses [1], while osimertinib features in dedicated prospective studies for advanced EGFR-positive disease [3]. Uncommon EGFR mutations receive focused examination in a survival outcomes trial [2]. Beyond small molecules, a chimeric antigen receptor T-cell construct co-targeting EGFR and B7H3 is under evaluation for lung and triple-negative breast malignancies [5]. Leptomeningeal disease complications are addressed through cerebrospinal fluid biomarker characterization during EGFR-TKI therapy [4].",
    "dur": 14.381
   },
